      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Makes sense. What about juggling multiple tasks\u2014any ADHD-friendly approaches for handling that?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to manage multiple tasks at work while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To effectively juggle multiple tasks with ADHD, consider these strategies:\n\n1. **Task Switching**: Alternate between two tasks throughout the day. This can help maintain focus and prevent boredom.\n\n2. **Break Tasks Down**: Divide larger projects into smaller, manageable steps. Set deadlines for each step to keep yourself on track.\n\n3. **Scheduled Tasks**: Instead of a general to-do list, schedule specific tasks for certain times. This structure can make tasks feel more achievable.\n\n4. **Use Tools for Focus**: Implement productivity tools like the Pomodoro Technique, which involves working for a set period followed by a short break.\n\n5. **Limit Multitasking**: While it might feel natural to juggle many things, focusing on one task at a time can often lead to better outcomes.\n\n6. **Incorporate Movement**: If you're feeling restless, incorporate short walks or physical activity between tasks to refresh your mind.\n\n7. **Visual Reminders**: Post deadlines and task lists where you can see them to keep your goals front and center.\n\nThese approaches can help you manage multiple tasks more effectively while accommodating your ADHD tendencies.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0045-19-12715:8": 1.6954810619354248,
            "clueweb22-en0036-05-19310:5": 1.6602215766906738,
            "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:4": 1.1434705257415771,
            "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:9": 0.8758460283279419,
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:11": 0.5778903961181641,
            "clueweb22-en0008-61-11890:2": 0.5406864881515503,
            "clueweb22-en0030-53-01118:15": 0.4475744962692261,
            "clueweb22-en0021-33-10264:6": 0.4349302649497986,
            "clueweb22-en0029-09-07368:2": 0.39045384526252747,
            "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": 0.3850727677345276,
            "clueweb22-en0008-99-02137:2": 0.24079760909080505,
            "clueweb22-en0045-19-12715:7": 0.13627691566944122,
            "clueweb22-en0021-33-10264:5": 0.09449896961450577,
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:7": 0.06212174892425537,
            "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:5": 0.03330173343420029,
            "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:11": 0.010015368461608887
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting! How does using a timer help someone with ADHD focus on just one thing?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Using a timer can significantly help someone with ADHD focus on a single task by creating a structured timeframe for work. Here\u2019s how it works:\n\n1. **Time Boundaries**: Setting a timer establishes a clear start and end for a task, which can help prevent drifting into distractions or hyperfocus on non-essential activities.\n\n2. **Chunking Work**: By breaking work into shorter intervals (like 15 or 30 minutes), it makes tasks feel more manageable and less overwhelming. This technique encourages sustained attention without the pressure of an endless work session.\n\n3. **Transition Cues**: A timer serves as a cue to transition between tasks or take breaks, which can help maintain overall productivity and reduce fatigue.\n\n4. **Physical Movement**: If you find yourself ignoring the timer due to hyperfocus, getting up to turn it off can serve as a physical break that helps reset your attention.\n\n5. **Visual Reminders**: Timers can be visual prompts that keep the task at hand in sight, reinforcing your commitment to completing it within the set timeframe.\n\nBy incorporating these strategies with a timer, you can create a more focused and productive work environment that accommodates your ADHD tendencies.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0039-55-00787:11": 1.5545353889465332,
            "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:3": 1.392616629600525,
            "clueweb22-en0030-53-01118:10": 1.0379992723464966,
