  {
    "runtag": "genaius-summary-rewrite",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm looking for ways to manage ADHD at work. What strategies are effective?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can ADHD be managed?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 4,
          "text": "Managing ADHD at work involves adopting strategies tailored to your unique challenges. Key approaches include minimizing distractions by using noise-canceling headphones or working in a quieter environment, maintaining an organized workspace to reduce visual clutter, and using tools like planners or digital reminders to stay on track. Breaking tasks into smaller, manageable steps and setting clear priorities can also help with focus and time management. Additionally, avoiding over-scheduling and building in regular breaks can prevent overwhelm. Seeking support from a career counselor or ADHD coach can provide personalized strategies and guidance. Remember, small adjustments can lead to significant improvements in productivity and workplace satisfaction.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:0": 15.55519962310791,
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:10": 14.05210018157959
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "What about if I have to work on many different tasks at once? Any tips for juggling them with ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to manage multiple tasks at work while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To juggle multiple tasks effectively with ADHD, focus on strategies that enhance executive functioning skills. Start by prioritizing tasks based on deadlines and importance\u2014this helps reduce overwhelm. Use tools like planners or digital apps to organize your tasks and set reminders. Break larger tasks into smaller, manageable steps to maintain focus and track progress. When switching between tasks, take a moment to refocus to minimize the cognitive load of task-switching. Additionally, consider using techniques like time-blocking, where you allocate specific periods for each task, ensuring dedicated focus. Regularly review your progress and adjust priorities as needed. Creating a structured routine can also help you stay organized and reduce decision fatigue. Since you recently moved for work, establishing a consistent workspace and routine in your new environment might further support your productivity. Lastly, don\u2019t hesitate to seek support or accommodations at work, such as flexible deadlines or task adjustments, to better align with your needs.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0026-81-04480:1": 27.762699127197266,
            "clueweb22-en0016-28-02016:5": 25.631099700927734,
            "clueweb22-en0015-08-19046:1": 23.193099975585938
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I recently moved places for my work.",
            "I work in an office."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Using a timer can be an effective tool for individuals with ADHD to focus on a single task. Timers provide a clear, external structure that helps create a sense of urgency and accountability. For example, visual timers, which show the passage of time through disappearing colors or moving sand, can make time more tangible and easier to track. This can be particularly helpful for those who struggle with \"time blindness,\" a common ADHD challenge where time feels abstract.\n\nThe Pomodoro Technique is a popular method that involves setting a timer for 25 minutes of focused work, followed by a 5-minute break. This approach breaks tasks into manageable chunks, making it easier to maintain attention and avoid burnout. Additionally, timers can be paired with scheduled breaks to prevent fatigue and maintain productivity over longer periods.\n\nBy using a timer, you can also consciously limit the time spent on tasks you enjoy, preventing hyperfocus on one activity at the expense of others. Over time, this practice can help improve time management and task completion. Experiment with different types of timers\u2014whether visual, auditory, or app-based\u2014to find what works best for your needs.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0011-76-08014:5": 18.861600875854492,
            "clueweb22-en0026-06-01592:6": 18.62350082397461,
            "clueweb22-en0001-66-11662:18": 18.517900466918945
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Handling distractions during hyperfocus can be challenging, but there are strategies to help manage this. First, set up external reminders, like alarms or timers, to periodically check in with your surroundings. This can help you break out of hyperfocus when necessary. Second, create a structured environment by minimizing potential distractions beforehand\u2014this might include silencing notifications or using noise-canceling headphones. Third, establish clear priorities and goals for your task so you can redirect your focus if you veer off track. Finally, practice mindfulness techniques to increase awareness of when you\u2019re becoming overly absorbed in a task. These strategies can help you maintain balance and stay productive without losing track of other responsibilities.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0022-33-08001:0": 21.15530014038086,
