          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yeah, I\u2019m interested! What are other tools aside from timers that can help with focus and organization at work?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Beyond timers, many adults with ADHD find digital to-do list apps like Evernote and Priority Matrix useful for capturing ideas, organizing tasks, and setting reminders [clueweb22-en0040-16-00993:1]. Project management tools such as Asana help break down projects into manageable steps and track progress [clueweb22-en0032-38-16716:0]. Fidget tools or small objects to keep your hands busy can improve focus during meetings or long work sessions [clueweb22-en0005-70-11136:3]. White noise machines or apps can help create a quiet environment, which is especially valuable if you love it when it is quiet and work in a shared office [clueweb22-en0032-38-16716:0]. Many people also use calendar apps with customizable alerts to manage appointments and deadlines, and some prefer physical tools like sticky notes or color-coded notebooks for visual organization [clueweb22-en0005-70-11136:2].",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-38-16716:0": 0.05498227262933146,
            "clueweb22-en0005-70-11136:3": 0.04381638376017471,
            "clueweb22-en0040-16-00993:1": 0.04298263594038242,
            "clueweb22-en0041-00-15995:0": 0.04153486311320852,
            "clueweb22-en0005-70-11136:2": 0.04042983528316163
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I work in an office.",
            "I love it when it is quiet.",
            "I have a sedentary lifestyle."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 9,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "This is great\u2014thank you!  \nWhat about when someone with ADHD is in hyperfocus? How can they handle distractions then, especially in an office?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Managing hyperfocus in an office setting involves setting clear boundaries before starting a task\u2014decide in advance how long you'll spend on it and use timers or alarms to signal when it's time to switch activities. Highly visible reminders, such as sticky notes or digital alerts, can help break your focus when necessary. If timers alone aren't enough, enlisting a coworker or accountability partner to check in or send a message can be effective. Mindfulness exercises and physically changing your position (like standing up or stretching) can also help you become aware of time passing and transition out of hyperfocus. These strategies not only help manage distractions but also allow you to harness the benefits of hyperfocus for productivity while maintaining balance in your workday [clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:7][clueweb22-en0007-71-00778:7][clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:5][clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:6]. Are there specific distractions you struggle with most during hyperfocus?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:7": 0.06299603174603174,
            "clueweb22-en0007-71-00778:7": 0.05768512533218416,
            "clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:5": 0.048915917503966164,
            "clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:6": 0.04839549075403121
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I work in an office.",
            "I love it when it is quiet."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 10,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yeah, sometimes people talking or sudden noises just pull me right out, even if I\u2019m super locked in. Any advice for dealing with those kinds of interruptions?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Sudden noises and conversations can be especially disruptive for people with ADHD, even during periods of hyperfocus. Many find that noise-cancelling headphones or earbuds, as well as white noise machines or apps, help create a consistent auditory environment that blocks out unpredictable sounds [clueweb22-en0032-38-16716:0]. Soft instrumental music or nature sounds can also mask background noise without being distracting. If you work in an office, using a visual cue\u2014like a 'do not disturb' sign or a colored object on your desk\u2014can signal to colleagues that you need uninterrupted time. Some people also schedule their most focus-intensive tasks during quieter office hours or use private meeting rooms when available. Combining these strategies can help you maintain your focus and reduce the impact of external interruptions [clueweb22-en0032-38-16716:0][clueweb22-en0005-70-11136:2]. Are you interested in strategies for communicating your needs to coworkers?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:7": 0.06299603174603174,
            "clueweb22-en0007-71-00778:7": 0.05768512533218416,
            "clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:5": 0.048915917503966164,
            "clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:6": 0.04839549075403121
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I love it when it is quiet.",
            "I work in an office."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 11,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "No, that\u2019s fine for now.  \nHonestly, even in quiet meetings, my mind wanders. Any ideas for staying present during long meetings?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "For adults with ADHD, staying focused in long, quiet meetings can be challenging due to natural tendencies toward daydreaming or restlessness. Active note-taking\u2014summarizing key points or jotting down questions\u2014can help anchor your attention. Doodling or sketching (which you may enjoy since you can draw well) can also keep your mind engaged without being disruptive. Using discreet fidget tools, like a small stress ball or textured object, can help channel excess energy and improve concentration. Setting a personal goal for each meeting, such as contributing at least once or listening for specific information, can provide structure. If possible, request meeting agendas in advance so you can prepare and stay oriented during discussions. These strategies can help you remain present and make meetings more productive and less tiring [clueweb22-en0005-70-11136:3][clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:7][clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:5]. Would you like tips on organizing your notes after meetings?",
          "citations": {},
