    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 10,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Yeah, sometimes people talking or sudden noises just pull me right out, even if I\u2019m super locked in. Any advice for dealing with those kinds of interruptions?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Sudden noises and conversations can be especially disruptive for people with ADHD, even during periods of hyperfocus. Many find that noise-cancelling headphones or earbuds, as well as white noise machines or apps, help create a consistent auditory environment that blocks out unpredictable sounds [clueweb22-en0032-38-16716:0]. Soft instrumental music or nature sounds can also mask background noise without being distracting. If you work in an office, using a visual cue\u2014like a 'do not disturb' sign or a colored object on your desk\u2014can signal to colleagues that you need uninterrupted time. Some people also schedule their most focus-intensive tasks during quieter office hours or use private meeting rooms when available. Combining these strategies can help you maintain your focus and reduce the impact of external interruptions [clueweb22-en0032-38-16716:0][clueweb22-en0005-70-11136:2]. Are you interested in strategies for communicating your needs to coworkers?",
          "citations": {
