          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting! How can someone utilize breaks effectively during work to manage ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 4,
          "text": "To use breaks effectively at work for managing ADHD, focus on strategies that restore alertness and support focus\u2014tailored to your office environment:\n\n**1. Movement Breaks**\n- Brief physical activity (stretching, walking, even fidgeting) boosts dopamine and noradrenaline, helping your brain reset and refocus. This is especially effective for ADHD, as movement compensates for underarousal and can improve attention. Even subtle movement at your desk is beneficial.\n\n**2. Sensory Stimulation**\n- Listening to music or background noise during breaks can enhance cognitive performance for people with ADHD. Choose upbeat or familiar music to lift mood and sharpen focus before returning to tasks.\n\n**3. Structured, Frequent Breaks**\n- Use timers to schedule short, regular breaks (e.g., 5 minutes every 25\u201330 minutes). This prevents mental fatigue and helps maintain consistent productivity.\n\n**4. Avoid Punitive Self-Talk**\n- Don\u2019t view the need for breaks or movement as a weakness; it\u2019s a proven self-regulation tool for ADHD.\n\nGiven your office setting, try",
          "citations": {
