  {
    "runtag": "orga-gpt41mini-bm25-minilm-llama70b",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I want to know more about managing ADHD at work. What are common strategies?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can ADHD be managed?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "You can try using \"white noise\" earphones or listening to classical music to drown out office noises. Alternatively, you can work in a quiet space, such as an unused conference room, or take work home when possible. Routing phone calls directly to voicemail and responding to them at a set time can also help minimize interruptions.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": 6.121223449707031,
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:10": 5.15645170211792,
            "clueweb22-en0002-41-06891:0": 3.7604756355285645,
            "clueweb22-en0014-93-12726:7": 3.6650607585906982,
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:0": 3.6202666759490967
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, but I already like it when it's quiet\u2014so I'm looking for more ways to manage ADHD generally, beyond just minimizing noise. What else is effective?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can ADHD be managed?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "In addition to minimizing noise, maintaining an organized space can help minimize distractions. Keeping notes can also be helpful, as individuals with ADHD are prone to forgetfulness. Avoiding over-scheduling and taking frequent breaks can also provide an outlet for symptoms of hyperactivity. Furthermore, rewarding yourself for completing tasks can provide an incentive to stay focused. These strategies can help you better manage your ADHD symptoms and improve your overall productivity.  Consider developing schedules with the help of a co-worker or supervisor to help with time management.  Regular exercise and healthy eating habits can also have a positive impact on managing ADHD symptoms.  By incorporating these strategies into your daily routine, you can better manage your ADHD and improve your overall well-being.  Consider keeping a notepad to jot down ideas and reminders to help stay organized and on track.  Taking the stairs instead of the elevator can also provide a brief break and some physical activity.  Using organizational tools such as planners and timers can also be helpful in staying organized and managing",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": 5.278909683227539,
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:10": 4.876657485961914,
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:11": 4.30074405670166,
            "clueweb22-en0014-93-12726:7": 4.034533977508545,
            "clueweb22-en0009-70-10081:11": 3.6685523986816406
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "These are great tips! I like the idea of keeping notes\u2014I already draw a lot, so maybe using sketch-notes would help me remember tasks better. Do you have examples of how to break up bigger tasks into smaller chunks for ADHD management?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to manage multiple tasks at work while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Breaking down big tasks into smaller, manageable chunks can be very helpful. For example, if you have a large project at work, you can break it down into smaller tasks like researching, outlining, drafting, and editing. This can make the task feel less overwhelming and allow you to focus on one step at a time. You can also use a task organizer or a to-do list to help you stay on track and visualize the smaller tasks. Additionally, you can set deadlines for each chunk and reward yourself when you complete them, which can help motivate you to stay focused. Using sketch-notes can be a great way to visualize these tasks and make them more memorable.   Please let me know if I can assist you further.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0005-60-16250:7": 4.535613059997559,
            "clueweb22-en0043-23-02572:7": 4.424883842468262,
            "clueweb22-en0011-48-04718:9": 4.365600109100342,
            "clueweb22-en0002-41-06891:7": 4.108642101287842,
            "clueweb22-en0001-49-07368:7": 3.806882381439209
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense, thanks! What about using timers? How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Using a timer can be a great way to help people with ADHD focus on a single task. The idea is to set a timer for a specific amount of time, say 25 or 30 minutes, and work on the task without any distractions during that time. This can help you stay focused and avoid multitasking. When the timer goes off, you can take a short break before starting the next chunk of work. This technique is often called the Pomodoro Technique. It can help you stay on track, avoid burnout, and make steady progress on your tasks. You can use a traditional kitchen timer or a mobile app to help you stay on track.   Let me know if you have any other questions!   (",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0030-53-01118:10": 5.920125484466553,
            "clueweb22-en0011-76-08014:6": 5.443225860595703,
            "clueweb22-en0038-68-03500:5": 5.407114505767822,
            "clueweb22-en0030-53-01118:9": 5.357948303222656,
            "clueweb22-en0001-66-11662:19": 5.0221052169799805
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Nice! I\u2019ve tried Pomodoro before, but sometimes when I\u2019m in hyperfocus, I ignore the timer. How can someone with ADHD handle distractions or reminders when they\u2019re deep in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "When in hyperfocus, it can be challenging to notice reminders or distractions. One strategy is to set multiple, highly visible reminders, such as alarms or enlisting someone to interrupt you. This can help you break free from hyperfocus and transition to other tasks. Additionally, having a partner, coach, or coworker to gently interrupt can be effective, as it's often harder to ignore a person than a timer. By using these strategies, you can learn to manage your hyperfocus and avoid getting stuck in a single task for too long.   Please let me know if I can assist you further.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0012-74-13153:6": 5.881998062133789,
            "clueweb22-en0036-30-15322:2": 3.720979690551758,
            "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": 3.4031076431274414,
            "clueweb22-en0016-28-02016:5": 2.893658399581909,
            "clueweb22-en0022-33-08001:0": 2.8625404834747314
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Good point\u2014I guess I could ask a coworker to ping me when meetings are about to start, since I sometimes get so into drawing during breaks that I lose track of time. Are there tools or apps that work well for reminding people with ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Yes, there are several tools and apps that can help people with ADHD stay on track and reminded. For example, you can use calendar apps with alert functions to set reminders for upcoming events or deadlines. Additionally, you can use apps that allow you to set timers and receive notifications when it's time to switch tasks or take a break. Some apps also offer features like customizable reminders, alerts, and notifications to help you stay organized and focused. You can explore different options to find the one that works best for you and your needs. Setting a timer while working on a project you enjoy can also help you stay on track and avoid losing time.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:3": 2.289860248565674,
            "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:2": 1.9234431982040405,
            "clueweb22-en0025-91-05722:4": 0.6374717950820923,
            "clueweb22-en0018-39-12322:7": 0.5020769238471985,
            "clueweb22-en0042-29-05763:2": -0.07830822467803955
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great, thanks! Sometimes in meetings though, I really space out, especially if they drag on. Are there techniques specifically for staying attentive in meetings with ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To stay attentive in meetings, you can try \"intentional fidgeting\" by taking notes or doodling, which can help you stay engaged without distracting others. You can also prepare ahead of time by reviewing the meeting agenda and setting specific goals for what you want to achieve or discuss. Additionally, sitting in a location where you can easily see the speaker and the visual aids can help you stay focused. If possible, taking a short break before the meeting to get some fresh air or stretch can also help increase your alertness and attention during the meeting. By using these strategies, you can stay more attentive and engaged during meetings, even with ADHD.   Please let me know if I can assist you further.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0012-26-00836:0": 4.18958854675293,
            "clueweb22-en0032-48-14323:7": 4.100895881652832,
