      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, I love it when it\u2019s quiet so the headphone tip is great for me. What about handling lots of tasks at once\u2014do you have ideas for multitasking or switching between tasks if you have ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to manage multiple tasks at work while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "When you have ADHD, it can be tempting to try to multitask or switch between tasks quickly, but research suggests that this can actually decrease productivity and increase stress. Instead, try using a strategy called single-tasking, where you focus on one task at a time. This can help you stay focused and avoid feeling overwhelmed. You can also try using a technique called the Pomodoro Technique, where you work on a task for a set amount of time (usually 25 minutes) and then take a break. This can help you stay focused and avoid burnout. Additionally, breaking tasks into smaller, more manageable steps can help you stay on track and avoid feeling overwhelmed. It may also be helpful to discuss your task management strategies with your employer or HR manager to see if there are any accommodations that can be made, such as working part-time or having a flexible schedule. Get enough sleep and practice good sleep hygiene to help you stay focused and alert during the day. Consider talking to your doctor or",
          "citations": {
