    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I want to know how ADHD can be managed at work. Any tips or tools?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can ADHD be managed?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here are practical, office \u2011 friendly ways to manage adhd at work : environment - request a quiet desk / office or use an empty conference room for deep work. - use noise \u2011 cancelling headphones or white noise ; close the door when possible. - route calls to voicemail and return them at a set time. focus and workflow - use short, timed sprints ( e. g., 20 \u2013 25 minutes ) with brief movement breaks. - keep a simple to \u2011 do list and a \u201c parking lot \u201d for ideas so daydreams don \u2019 t derail tasks. - limit social media with site blockers during focus blocks. - batch email / messages ; turn off nonessential notifications. communication and supports - ask for instructions both verbally and in writing ; get permission to record key directions. - request extra time to learn complex tasks. - seek regular, constructive feedback ; consider a coach or accountability partner. tools - scheduling / task apps ( calendar + checklists ), kanban boards, reminders. - headphones, note \u2011 taking tools, and voice memos. self \u2011 management - brief walks or stretches to manage restlessness and fatigue. - mindfulness training ( including virtual, non \u2011 religious programs",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0038-05-08753:8": -1.4536004066467285,
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": -1.5288678407669067,
            "clueweb22-en0017-60-15663:1": -1.882014274597168,
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:9": -2.0102169513702393,
            "clueweb22-en0007-26-03202:4": -2.281921863555908
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Awesome, thanks! Are there specific task management methods that work better for people with ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to manage multiple tasks at work while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "yes. many adhd - friendly methods reduce overwhelm and make \u201c starting \u201d friction tiny. - kanban with wip limits : use a simple board ( backlog, today, doing, done ). limit doing to 1 \u2013 3 items to prevent task \u2011 hopping. - timeboxing / flow sprints : work in 15 \u2013 40 minute sprints with a visible timer, then take a short movement break. adjust length to your focus that day. - good \u2013 better \u2013 best goals : define three acceptable finish lines before you start ( minimum viable, solid, ideal ). this fights perfectionism and lets you stop at \u201c good \u201d when needed. - 5 \u2011 minute gateway : commit to just five minutes on a task. once you \u2019 re moving, extend if momentum appears. - mits + parking lot : pick 1 \u2013 3 most important tasks for today. capture every stray idea in a parking lot list so daydreams don \u2019 t derail you. - body doubling : co \u2011 work ( in person or virtual ) to create gentle accountability. - if \u2013 then scripts and checklists : predecide next steps ( if x happens, then i do y ). use templates for recurring tasks. - energy \u2011 based",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0013-53-01247:1": -5.873078346252441,
            "clueweb22-en0032-34-16977:6": -7.216206073760986,
            "clueweb22-en0014-64-02464:6": -7.402561664581299,
            "clueweb22-en0009-68-00215:5": -7.470597267150879,
            "clueweb22-en0005-22-11531:17": -7.573644161224365
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting! How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "timers help by externalizing time \u2014 creating a visible, finite container for one task \u2014 so you feel urgency to start and a clear permission to stop. that reduces time blindness, task \u2011 switching, and perfectionism. how to use one - task sprints - choose one task. set a visual or silent timer for 15 \u2013 30 minutes ( adjust daily ). - make an if \u2013 then rule : \u201c if the timer is running, i don \u2019 t switch tasks. \u201d - turn off notifications ; park new ideas on a \u201c parking lot \u201d list. - work until it ends ; take a 3 \u2013 5 minute movement break. repeat 2 \u2013 4 cycles, then a longer break. when you \u2019 re stuck - 5 \u2011 minute gateway : set just 5 minutes to break inertia. - good \u2013 better \u2013 best : predefine acceptable stopping points ; stop at \u201c good \u201d when the timer ends. timer types that suit quiet offices - visual \u201c red \u2011 disk \u201d timers : great for time awareness without sound. - flip cube timers ( pre \u2011 set 5 / 15 / 25 / 30 ) : tiny start friction. - large led magnetic timers with a flashing or low \u2011 volume alarm. - phone / smartwatch vibration",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0011-22-13024:0": -0.27163830399513245,
            "clueweb22-en0019-48-01172:0": -0.7149800062179565,
            "clueweb22-en0015-62-05871:0": -0.7368864417076111,
            "clueweb22-en0046-35-06149:0": -0.896363377571106,
            "clueweb22-en0044-25-03068:4": -2.053745746612549
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense! What about hyperfocus, though? How can someone with ADHD avoid getting sucked into one thing and missing other deadlines?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "hyperfocus is powerful \u2014 use it with guardrails so you don \u2019 t blow past other commitments. - pre \u2011 plan hard stops - block tasks in your calendar with alarms : 10 \u2011 min warning, stop time, and a 2 \u2011 min \u201c nag. \u201d - use a silent visual timer in sight ( red \u2011 disk or traffic \u2011 light style ). pair with phone / watch vibration so it \u2019 s quiet. - write the next appointment / deadline on a small card next to the timer as a visual anchor. - mini \u2011 deadlines - break work into 20 \u2013 40 min chunks with clear deliverables. put brief review check \u2011 ins with a teammate on the calendar to force switching. - exit ritual ( when the timer warns ) - save work, jot the very next step, schedule the next session, move the task to \u201c parked, \u201d close tabs / apps. then stand, stretch, sip water, or eat your apple. - priority guard - keep a tiny \u201c hard stops today ( 1 \u2013 3 ) \u201d list with times. everything else goes to a parking lot. - anti \u2011 rabbit \u2011 hole tools - site / app blockers during focus blocks ; optional auto \u2011 close at stop time. - energy",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0015-62-05871:0": -0.6385230422019958,
            "clueweb22-en0040-95-12776:1": -2.293116569519043,
            "clueweb22-en0029-36-12879:0": -2.7676353454589844,
            "clueweb22-en0032-01-09532:1": -2.8570520877838135,
            "clueweb22-en0014-17-07331:0": -3.9146833419799805
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful! Are there any tricks for staying attentive in meetings if you have ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "here are meeting - focused strategies that tend to work well with adhd : before - get the agenda and materials in advance ; highlight your questions and desired outcomes. - do a 2 \u2013 3 minute brisk walk or stretch to boost alertness ; arrive a bit early to settle. - set your phone / watch to do not disturb ; route calls to voicemail. during - sit near the speaker, back to windows / doors to reduce visual noise. - use a simple notes template : decisions, action items ( who / when ), questions, parking lot. - jot ideas as they pop up so daydreams don \u2019 t derail you ; light doodling can help focus. - use a discreet fidget, water, or sugar - free gum ; if drowsy, stand at the back briefly. - set subtle watch vibrations every 10 \u2013 15 minutes as \u201c attention checks. \u201d - in virtual meetings : full - screen the speaker, hide self - view, turn off notifications. after - summarize action items and next steps in a quick message to confirm understanding. - if allowed, record key instructions ; ask for written follow - up of decisions. if meetings are long / frequent, consider accommodations : agendas in advance,",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:9": 0.01878988742828369,
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:10": -0.5246213674545288,
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": -0.8307926654815674,
            "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:0": -1.961120367050171,
            "clueweb22-en0017-60-15663:0": -2.3335442543029785
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you for sharing all these excellent, practical strategies! If you need more resources, have any other questions, or would like tailored suggestions, feel free to ask. Otherwise, best of luck putting these ADHD-friendly tools to use at work. Take care and farewell!",
          "user_rubrik": null,
