  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini-nopersonal",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, I want to know how ADHD can be managed, especially at work. What are some common approaches?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can ADHD be managed?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "you can manage adhd at work with a mix of medical, behavioral, environmental, and workplace strategies. key approaches : - seek assessment and treatment : a formal diagnosis lets you pursue medication ( stimulants or non \u2011 stimulants ) and evidence - based therapies. medication often reduces core symptoms ; therapy ( cbt, coaching, mindfulness ) improves skills and coping. - structure and planning : break tasks into small steps, use checklists, prioritize with a simple system ( e. g., top 3 tasks ), set clear deadlines and use timers ( pomodoro ). - reduce distractions : use noise - cancelling headphones, a quiet workspace, single - task when possible, and limit digital interruptions ( notifications off, website blockers ). - external supports and reminders : calendar alerts, task apps, visual cues, and written instructions help with forgetfulness. - sleep and self - care : good sleep hygiene matters \u2014 address sleep timing issues and treat co - occurring anxiety / depression if present. - workplace accommodations : ask for flexible scheduling, clear written assignments, regular check \u2011 ins, or job coaching. you don \u2019 t have to manage this alone \u2014",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0022-36-16118:13": 27.393299102783203,
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:0": 26.571399688720703,
            "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:1": 26.50149917602539,
            "clueweb22-en0026-24-08399:10": 26.426000595092773,
            "clueweb22-en0027-96-10976:1": 25.827899932861328
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Oh, using checklists and breaking tasks down sounds helpful. Is there a specific way to prioritize tasks that works well for ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to manage multiple tasks at work while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 ADHD-friendly prioritizing is best when it\u2019s simple, consistent, and tied to deadlines, value, and your energy. Try this routine:  1. Capture everything in one master list (brain dump).   2. Triage each item using three quick questions:      - Time: Is there a hard deadline?      - Impact: Which task moves you closer to a key goal or prevents a big problem?      - Effort/Energy: Can it be done in 5\u201315 minutes or does it need a deep-focus block?   3. Choose your daily \"Top 3\" (one urgent, one high-impact, one small/quick win). Do those first, ideally in your peak-focus window.   4. Break each top item into tiny, timed steps (Pomodoro or 15\u2011minute sprints) and use a visible checklist.   5. Review weekly to move tasks between categories and adjust priorities.   6. Use external supports: calendar alerts, color-coding, and an accountability partner or coach.  Keep the system tiny and repeatable \u2014 consistency beats complexity.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0026-81-04480:1": 31.02720069885254,
            "clueweb22-en0021-16-10328:0": 29.26099967956543,
            "clueweb22-en0033-15-10365:0": 28.89080047607422,
            "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:0": 28.617300033569336,
            "clueweb22-en0002-13-04194:0": 27.762300491333008
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks! I'm curious about the timer part\u2014how does using a timer actually help someone with ADHD focus on one task? Does it make a real difference, or is it just a reminder?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Timers do more than remind \u2014 they change how your brain treats a task. For people with ADHD they work by externalizing time, creating mini-deadlines and predictable start/stop cues that reduce overwhelm and decision-fatigue. That does several helpful things: it boosts urgency (which increases motivation and dopamine), limits hyperfocus or \u201cattentional blinks\u201d by forcing breaks, chunks large tasks into doable steps, and gives you frequent small wins that reinforce continued effort.  How to use them: - Try Pomodoro (25 min work / 5 min break) or shorter sprints (15\u201317 min) if 25 feels long. - Do Top\u20113 items in your peak-focus window. - Use a visible countdown, vibrating timer, or app that locks distractions. - Pair each sprint with a one-line checklist and a tiny reward at completion.  If one format feels hard, shorten the work interval. Consistency matters more than perfect timing.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0028-83-09604:0": 32.935699462890625,
            "clueweb22-en0015-27-05231:10": 32.402000427246094,
            "clueweb22-en0028-41-09433:0": 32.1593017578125,
            "clueweb22-en0019-77-06418:0": 32.03200149536133,
            "clueweb22-en0026-87-14626:11": 31.766599655151367
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Interesting! What about when someone with ADHD is in hyperfocus\u2014like, so deep into work they lose track of everything else? How can they handle distractions in that state?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "hyperfocus can be great for deep work \u2014 but it \u2019 s easy to lose track of time and other responsibilities. practical ways to manage it : before you dive - set a clear intention : pick the exact task and the outcome ( rule of 3, or one \u201c most consequential \u201d task ). - pre \u2011 commit an end time and checkpoints ( e. g., \u201c work 50 minutes, stop at 11 : 00 \u201d ). while you \u2019 re in it - use external time anchors : visible countdown timers, vibrating alarms, or an hourly chime so you physically notice time passing. - build \u201c if / then \u201d rules : \u201c if the alarm rings, i \u2019 ll stop and check my calendar. \u201d - limit interruptions to true urgencies ( use dnd but allow calls from one emergency contact ). - put a one - line checklist or a sticky note with your next obligation where you \u2019 ll see it when you look up. after a session - capture any stray ideas into a single inbox ( so you can let them go ) and set a follow - up reminder. - do a quick 2 \u2011 minute review to decide next steps. small, external cues and pre \u2011 decisions are what break",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0022-21-03294:10": 45.19620132446289,
            "clueweb22-en0022-21-03294:11": 42.95240020751953,
            "clueweb22-en0041-12-03522:5": 40.83769989013672,
            "clueweb22-en0022-33-08001:0": 40.44430160522461,
            "clueweb22-en0029-41-01120:0": 39.22779846191406
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense! Speaking of meetings\u2014how can someone with ADHD stay attentive when there\u2019s a long or boring meeting? That\u2019s super tough for me sometimes, especially if there are a lot of people around. Any strategies?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Good question \u2014 meetings can be brutal for ADHD, but small tactics make a big difference. Try a mix of preparation, in\u2011meeting supports, and environment tweaks:  - Set a clear meeting goal (your Top 1\u20133): what you\u2019ll listen for or one thing you\u2019ll contribute. That gives your attention a purpose. - Prep 1\u20133 written questions or things to note beforehand so you\u2019re actively searching for answers. - Use headphones or noise-cancelling earbuds to reduce background noise and help you stay present. - Externalize time: set a visible timer or checkpoint (e.g., \u201ccheck in at 20 minutes\u201d) so you don\u2019t lose track. - Use discreet stims/fidgets (stress ball, footrest, silent spinner) or shift posture; if audible, mute your mic or use vibration alerts. - Capture stray thoughts quickly in a single inbox note so you can let them go and return focus. - Ask for reasonable accommodations when needed (clear agenda, off\u2011camera option, scheduled breaks, or written follow-ups).  Small, consistent habits matter more than perfect focus \u2014 pick one or two to try and iterate.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0044-55-00401:7": 35.40480041503906,
            "clueweb22-en0030-63-11209:8": 32.79059982299805,
            "clueweb22-en0008-36-12963:0": 31.94569969177246,
            "clueweb22-en0044-55-00401:6": 30.835500717163086,
            "clueweb22-en0019-58-01043:7": 30.32900047302246
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
