  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s super useful! So about timers\u2014how does using one actually help someone with ADHD focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "A timer works for ADHD because it externalizes time, removes the need to hold deadlines or durations in working memory, and turns vague work into a clearly bounded, doable chunk.  Why that helps: - Limits overwhelm: \u201cI only need to focus for 15\u201325 minutes\u201d makes starting much easier. - Creates urgency and structure: a time box reduces perfectionist rabbit holes and endless tweaking. - Reduces distraction cost: you promise yourself to ignore interruptions until the timer rings, so switching costs go down. - Improves re\u2011engagement: knowing a break is coming makes it easier to concentrate during the period. - Supports energy management: short bursts let you match work to when you\u2019re alert and schedule movement or brief naps when tired.  How to try it: start with 15 minutes, use a visible or silent visual timer, take a short break when it ends, and increase session length as it gets easier. Combine with checklists or a body\u2011double for extra accountability.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0019-48-01172:0": -0.4975787401199341,
            "clueweb22-en0012-91-02398:18": -0.5271868705749512,
            "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:5": -0.538193941116333,
            "clueweb22-en0030-54-15087:19": -1.2913649082183838,
            "clueweb22-en0012-51-00120:11": -1.3053147792816162
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      }
    ],
    "judgment": {
      "subtopic-justification": "What is a body-double?",
      "engagement": "Functional Tool",
      "relevance-and-usefulness": "Moderately Meets",
      "overall-subtopic-quality": "Good",
      "rater-confidence": "High confidence"
    }
  },
  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Nice! And when someone is in hyperfocus mode, do they even notice outside distractions? What helps break out of that if they need to?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 hyperfocus often makes you effectively oblivious to outside distractions and internal prompts; logical self-talk or trying to willfully switch usually won\u2019t work. The most reliable fixes are external and pre-planned.  Practical steps you can try: - Pre-set a visible/alarm timer to go off a few minutes before you need to stop (15\u201325 min works).   - Use a partner, coach, or coworker to gently interrupt you at set times (an agreed nudge beats an internal struggle).   - Create an \u201curgent\u201d channel (call/instant message) so only truly time\u2011sensitive things get through; batch email checks.   - Build a short transition ritual the moment you\u2019re interrupted (stand, drink water, walk 2 minutes) to force your brain to shift focus.   - If hyperfocus becomes distressing, use a stronger external interrupt (phone vibration, bright light, or a call) and consider discussing routines or accommodations with HR or a clinician.  These external cues make disengaging much easier than trying to rely on willpower alone.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0045-19-12715:5": 0.7212631702423096,
            "clueweb22-en0032-61-13276:14": 0.27856189012527466,
            "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:7": 0.05302947014570236,
            "clueweb22-en0036-30-15322:1": -0.15827788412570953,
            "clueweb22-en0036-30-15322:2": -0.30494600534439087
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      }
    ],
    "judgment": {
      "subtopic-justification": "Using external cues rather than relying on willpower alone was a personal comment.",
      "engagement": "Functional Tool",
      "relevance-and-usefulness": "Moderately Meets",
      "overall-subtopic-quality": "Acceptable",
      "rater-confidence": "High confidence"
    }
  },
  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. For meetings\u2014what\u2019s a top trick to stay attentive during long group calls when you have ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Top trick: give your attention a job \u2014 make the meeting do something useful for your brain.  How to do it (quick, practical): - Claim a role: volunteer to be timekeeper, note\u2011taker, or action\u2011item recorder. Purpose keeps you engaged. - Use a visible timer with checkpoints (e.g., 25/5 or simple 15\u2011minute markers) so you have predictable moments to refocus or stand. - Turn note\u2011taking into sketch\u2011notes or doodles (you\u2019re good at drawing): visual notes anchor attention without needing to stare at every speaker. - Keep a quiet fidget (or stand/move discreetly) and use noise\u2011reducing headphones if background noise distracts you. - Ask for a short agenda and defined outcomes up front so you know when to tune in and when to skim.  Those external cues (role, timer, visual notes) beat trying to will yourself to pay attention.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0018-75-17093:5": -3.11086368560791,
            "clueweb22-en0028-83-09604:0": -3.2462210655212402,
            "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:2": -3.409510612487793,
            "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:1": -3.667686700820923,
            "clueweb22-en0032-22-09151:15": -3.7361721992492676
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      }
    ],
    "judgment": {
      "subtopic-justification": "Good at drawing is the first time PTKB mentioned.",
      "engagement": "Functional Tool",
      "relevance-and-usefulness": "Moderately Meets",
      "overall-subtopic-quality": "Acceptable",
      "rater-confidence": "High confidence"
    }
  },
  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-no-no-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "8-1",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm looking for ways ADHD can be managed, especially at work. Any tips?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can ADHD be managed?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "nice question \u2014 practical, small changes make big differences. quick strategies you can try at work : - structure : break tasks into 10 \u2013 30 minute chunks, use a visible daily priority list and start with the easiest / most important. - time tools : pomodoro timer, calendar blocks, reminders and apps ( todoist, trello, forest ). - environment : ask for a quiet workspace or noise - cancelling headphones ( you value quiet ). reduce visual clutter ; keep one \u201c active \u201d project area. - sensory / movement : schedule short movement breaks ( walk, stretch, standing desk, fidget toy ). you mentioned being able to nap anywhere \u2014 a 10 \u2013 20 min rest can reset focus. - routines : regular sleep, meals and a short morning ritual to anchor the day ( you already have the apple habit ). - creative outlets : use quick sketches or ink studies to process ideas or as focused breaks \u2014 leverage your drawing / art strengths. - communication : request reasonable accommodations ( flexible hours, quieter room ) and set clear expectations with teammates. - professional help : consider adhd assessment, medication or cbt, and occupational therapy ; local support groups or a psychiatrist can help in germany. try one",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Breaking tasks into smaller chunks sounds like something I could do! I have a whiteboard at my desk, maybe listing 3 things max would help? Are there ways to stay motivated if I lose interest halfway?",
          "user_rubrik": "How to manage multiple tasks at work while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 3 things on the whiteboard is perfect. A few concrete ways to stay motivated when interest fades:  - Break each of the 3 items into very small visible substeps (10\u201330 min). Check boxes give quick wins. - Timebox with a Pomodoro (25/5) or a single 10\u2013minute sprint; often starting is the hardest part. - Use an \u201cif-then\u201d restart plan: if I lose interest, then I\u2019ll do 2 minutes of the next smallest step or take a 10\u201320 min power nap to reset. - Add a tiny reward after completion (tea, 5\u2011minute sketch, a quick thrift-shop browse online). - Swap mode: when bored, switch to a related creative task (sketching an idea) to keep momentum. - Accountability: tell a colleague or use a check-in app for external pressure. - Reduce friction: keep only one active project area on your desk and use noise-cancelling headphones for quiet focus.  Try one change at a time and keep what actually helps you.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I like that \u201ctiny reward\u201d tip\u2014maybe drawing a little sketch after finishing a step. How do timers actually help people with ADHD stay on track for a single task though?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a timer help people with ADHD to focus on a single task?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Timers help by externalizing time and turning vague stretches into clear, short commitments \u2014 which directly counters time blindness and initiation trouble common in ADHD. A few mechanisms:  - Reduces the start barrier: \u201cI only need to work for 10\u201325 minutes\u201d makes beginning easier. - Creates urgency and focus: a visible countdown narrows attention and limits perfectionism. - Encourages regular resets: built-in breaks lower fatigue and restore motivation (good for dopamine dips). - Provides immediate feedback and tiny wins: finishing a timed block feels like progress.  Practical tips: pick a session length that feels doable (try 10\u201315 min sprints or Pomodoro 25/5), use a visible timer or quiet vibration/alarm if you value silence, set a single micro-goal for the block, and pair completion with a tiny reward (a quick 5\u2011minute sketch). If interest fades, use a 2\u2011minute restart rule: start just two minutes and often you\u2019ll continue. Adjust lengths until it fits your rhythm.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I usually forget about time completely when I\u2019m hyperfocused and miss breaks. How can someone with ADHD remember to actually take breaks when in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik": "How can a person with ADHD handle distractions while in hyperfocus?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Good question \u2014 hyperfocus makes breaks easy to miss. Practical, low-friction tactics:  - Pre-commit and automate: schedule repeating break alerts in your calendar or use a Pomodoro app that vibrates or shows a silent banner (use vibration if you value quiet).   - Make the cue unavoidable: set the alarm across the room or on a smartwatch so you must get up to turn it off. Movement breaks are more likely if you have to stand.   - Create a tiny break ritual: one stretch + refill water or eat a slice of your apple + a 1\u20132 minute sketch. Small rituals make pausing attractive.   - Use an \u201cif-then\u201d rule: if the timer rings and I\u2019m still working, then I\u2019ll do exactly one micro-action (stand, walk 30 steps, or sketch for 3 minutes) before deciding to continue.   - Accountability nudge: tell a colleague or set shared check-ins so someone prompts you gently.   - Environment change: move to another seat or open a window to mark the break.    Try one change at a time and keep what actually pulls you out of flow.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That \u201ctiny break ritual\u201d actually sounds fun, especially stretching or sketching. Do people with ADHD get more tired from meetings, or is that just me? I feel drained after group calls sometimes.",
          "user_rubrik": "How to stay attentive in meetings while having ADHD?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "You\u2019re not imagining it \u2014 many people with ADHD find group calls especially draining. Reasons it\u2019s tiring: - Sustained attention to others\u2019 speech, slides and chat is effortful and depletes cognitive resources.   - Social processing (reading cues, worrying about how you come across) adds extra emotional load.   - Sensory/notification overload and frequent context switches increase fatigue.   - Long passive meetings remove movement and reinforcement that help maintain focus, so you burn energy trying to stay engaged.  Practical tweaks you can try: - Shorten or split meetings; ask for agendas and clear goals.   - Turn camera off when okay, or use a small sketch/notetaking ritual to anchor attention.   - Stand, stretch, or walk during calls (hands-free) to reduce restlessness.   - Batch meetings into specific times and leave buffer for a short reset (walk, water, 5\u2011minute sketch).   - Use polite accommodations: \u201cI focus best in short meetings \u2014 can we limit to 30 min?\u201d    Small changes make a big difference \u2014 try one and see how your energy shifts.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you so much for your thoughtful advice and practical strategies! I really appreciate your help and insights. Wishing you all the best\u2014take care and farewell!",
          "user_rubrik": null,
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": null,
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      }
    ],
    "judgment": {
      "dialog-justification": "There are no citations so user might not trust the answers.",
      "trustworthiness": "Unreliable",
      "mixed-initiative-strategies": "Competent & Proactive",
      "personalization": "Successfully Personalized",
      "information-flow-and-coherence": "Seamless & Logical",
      "overall-user-satisfaction": "Task Partially Met",
      "rater-confidence": "High confidence"
    }
  },
